## Blog Entries

- Blog lives in **paradigm-threat-files** (`blog/`), not this repo. Add entries there. See `docs/BLOG.md` in paradigm-threat-files.
- All links and images in blog files must use **fully absolute URLs** — `https://paradigmthreat.net/path`.
- **Cover all changed repos and list all changes — git first, always.** Before writing a blog entry: (1) **Required:** run **`git log`** in all three **paradigm-threat\*** repos since the **previous** dated `blog/YYYY-MM-DD-*.md` in paradigm-threat-files (see **paradigm-threat-files `docs/BLOG.md`** § *Git history* for cutoff and example commands). Do not draft from chat memory alone. (2) Target **~2×** the length of typical 2026 roundups (**~100–170 lines**); see **`docs/BLOG.md`** § *Length and depth*. (3) Start each repo section with a **two- or three-paragraph summary** (themes, connections, reader impact); use **thematic H3s** for large files-repo clusters. (4) List every new or updated item with a **link** and **one to three sentences** on what it covers — timeline: `https://paradigmthreat.net/timeline/evt-<id>`, site: `https://paradigmthreat.net/<path>`. (5) Group by repo; omit a repo section only if it had no changes. Add a **reading order** when multiple narrative lanes shipped.

## Timeline book voice (`content/**/*.md`)

- **Emphasis:** In `content/*.md`, use `**bold**` and `*italic*` sparingly for stress—about **one to four** emphasized spans per paragraph on average—not word-by-word highlighting. See `docs/CONTENT_MARKDOWN_STYLE.md`. (Does not apply outside `content/`.)
- **No meta-labels** in running prose: avoid "author thesis," "author only," "Paradigm Threat theory," "this site's reading," and similar unless a rare footnote is explicitly requested.
- **Show the logic** when extending beyond a named source (e.g. Fomenko): state **what the source covers** (e.g. horizon ~11th c. forward), **what the chronology establishes elsewhere** (e.g. Istanbul church predates Christ), **therefore** the next inference. Write in **third person** or neutral **"this chronology holds"** — book-facing, not editorial self-reference.
- **Does not apply** to `paradigm-threat-files` or blog copy unless editing those for another reason.

## TL;DR for long `content/` articles

- **When:** More than **12 paragraph-blocks** after YAML frontmatter (blank-line separated non-empty blocks; see `docs/CONTENT_STRATEGY.md` § TL;DR).
- **Where:** Right under `#` title, or under the hero image if one sits directly below the title.
- **Format:** `> **TL;DR:** TODO` until a model pass replaces `TODO` with one paragraph (see `docs/CONTENT_STRATEGY.md` § TL;DR).
- **Tooling:** `npm run generate-tldr` on `content/`; `python3 scripts/generate_tldr.py --root ../paradigm-threat-files`; `--refresh-tldr` resets to `TODO`.

## Print-oriented editions (`content-editions/`)

- **Two book products:** definitive **`content/`** (default + educational, one tree), **`content-editions/modern-religious/`** (religious). Edition trees are not fed to `events.json`. See **docs/BOOK_EDITIONS_PLAN.md**. No separate `content-editions/jewish/` tree.

## File Conventions

- Content markdown: `content/<chapter>/XX.YY.ZZ-slug.md`. **content/ is the source of truth**; events.json is generated from it.
- Title is taken from the first `#` header in the markdown.
- Dates: include in filename slug when present (e.g. `1664-1694`, `1774-ce`, `4077-bce`).
- Optional fields (media, related_events, duplicate_of): use YAML frontmatter.

## Image Generation (Golden Age vs Dark Age)

When generating or replacing illustrations for timeline articles:

### Book Illustrations (`.book-illustration`)

- **Aspect ratio:** Use **wide horizontal format** — limited height, full page width. Aim for ~3:1 (e.g. 1357×476 px) so illustrations fit the 6″×9″ print layout. Use taller proportions only when the illustration requires it.

- **Resolution:** Target ~1360×480 px for wide diagrams (or equivalent in vector). This is the preferred size for book illustrations.

- **Format preference:** Prefer **vector formats (SVG)** over raster (PNG, JPG) — SVG scales cleanly and suits diagrams, cosmologies, and technical illustrations. Use raster (PNG/JPG) when depicting **human scenes** (figures, faces, narrative art) where vector would look flat or artificial.

- **SVG diagrams:** Use **greyscale only** (no color). **Opaque backgrounds are okay** when the diagram has text, lines, or illustrations — use book-page cream (#f5f4f0) so content is visible in both light and dark mode. Text and strokes must be dark (#111–#444) to read clearly. Grid lines and fills may use light greys (#ccc, #ddd) with low opacity where needed.

- **Color and detail:** For raster images (human scenes), use full color and sufficient detail. For SVG diagrams, use greyscale only. Avoid sparse or overly minimal diagrams unless the content demands it.

- **Key table:** Include a **key/legend** when the illustration has multiple labeled elements, phases, or symbols that readers need to identify. Omit the key when the diagram is self-explanatory or labels are sufficient.

- **Saturn’s rings:** Saturn does **not** have rings in BCE times, only in CE. In BCE/Golden Age images, do not draw Saturn with the distinct ring system; use a sunlike body, spoked wheel, or plasma halo/Absu only.

- **Venus vs Mars (Wheel of Heaven):** In Golden Age / BCE images, **Venus** is a **plasmoid** appearing as a **single** star figure (one 3–8 sided star, e.g. six- or eight-pointed)—luminous, plasma-like—not many stars or symbols. **Mars** always looks **solid** (a distinct spherical or solid body, not plasmoid). Do not show other symbols in the sky; only Mars, Venus (single star), and Saturn.

- **Golden Age (and before-creation / creation-era):** Always use **Saturnian Cosmology** backgrounds showing the **Wheel of Heaven**. The **northern hemisphere** looked like the Northern Hemisphere Configuration: **Saturn + Venus + Mars = Tree of Life**—central luminous body (Saturn as sunlike, no rings), polar view “up the magnetic tube,” central spoked wheel, eight-pointed Sun Wheel / Shamash-style motif, Dharmachakra-style wheel; plasma halo or Absu (no distinct planetary rings). Sky can transition from deep blue to hazy yellowish-orange near the horizon. The **southern hemisphere** looked like the Southern Hemisphere Configuration: **petroglyph images in the sky**—Uranus + Neptune + Mercury forming the squatting-man figure; purple-tinged, plasma-like sky with luminous swirls; white or glowing stylized petroglyph figures (anthropomorphic, zoomorphic, squatting-man, Kokopelli-style) superimposed in the sky as if ancient rock art were celestial; barren rocky landscape with ancient observers. This applies to any image set in or before the Golden Age.

- **Dark Age:** Do **not** show the Wheel of Heaven—it is no longer present. Instead show **Venus and Mars tethered together as the dragon** (the new celestial configuration of the Dark Age). No Saturnian polar wheel or sunlike Saturn in the sky.
